<p>Sal <del><a href="https://sals.place/blog/moving-to-11ty-and-cloudflare/">is moving</a></del> (<a href="https://sals.place/blog/blog-move-successful/">has moved</a>) his blog off of Bear to an independent repository on CloudFlare, and that caused recurring concerns with Micro.blog.</p>
<p>As Sal said about Bear, I have nothing but good things to say about Micro.blog. Really. I love it here, and I recommend Micro.blog to anyone who seems to be slightly passionate about starting a blog. I think it&rsquo;s unique in terms of what it provides and in terms of a positive community, and it comes with a suite of complementary apps that enhance blogging as an experience. But.</p>
<p>As the saying goes, it&rsquo;s not a matter of if but a matter of when. And, as far as I know, Micro. blog doesn&rsquo;t have a contingency plan (is that what you call it?).</p>
<p>Personally, as long as Micro.blog offers an option to export data (which I do regularly, as well as backing up my blog) I think I&rsquo;ll be OK - and I don&rsquo;t believe it will ever be turned off. There are other options if something happens, and I could self-host my own site again if I have to, but as Sal mentioned in his post: &ldquo;I&rsquo;d wager that most bloggers would rather not bother and would much prefer a platform like Bear to take care of all the nuts and bolts.&rdquo;</p>
<p>Yep. Been there, done that. There&rsquo;s more than enough tech related stuff I do around my website (if you&rsquo;ve been around for more than a month you&rsquo;ve probably encountered one or two) as is. Besides, I also like the <em>idea</em> of Micro.blog, not just what it does. Paying for it as a service feels good. It&rsquo;s part of the &ldquo;good internet&rdquo; the way I see it.</p>
<p>Oh well, the good news is that maybe I&rsquo;ll get to read more posts from Sal, now that he can use his work laptop 😁</p>
